WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So your book,
"Outside the Wire,"

00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:04.170 size:88% position:44%
references when you were
serving in Afghanistan

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.840 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you would leave the base
to go talk to people.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.542
You were also, after you
returned from Afghanistan,

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:10.910
27 years old when you were
elected to state office.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:12.278 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.

00:00:12.278 --> 00:00:16.216 align:start size:94% position:6%
-What was it like being
that young in the statehouse?

00:00:16.216 --> 00:00:18.651 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, so this is the oldest
I´ve ever been.

00:00:18.651 --> 00:00:21.654 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
So we´ll start there.

00:00:21.654 --> 00:00:25.125
I got to Jefferson City, and
people would always ask me --

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:27.394 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think they meant,
"Who do you intern for?"

00:00:27.394 --> 00:00:29.162 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they would say,
"Who do you work for?"

00:00:29.162 --> 00:00:32.098 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I would say,
"35,000 Kansas Citians."

00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:35.402
And, you know, from there,
every office I´ve ever run for,

00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:38.038
I´ve kind of been the young guy
running for it.

00:00:38.038 --> 00:00:39.339
So I guess, what was it like?

00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:42.008 align:start size:94% position:6%
I helped a lot of people
with their smartphones and...

00:00:42.008 --> 00:00:43.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:43.877 --> 00:00:47.247 align:start size:94% position:6%
I explained Twitter.
-That´s nice.

00:00:47.247 --> 00:00:49.215
Do you look back and say,
"That was a terrible thing

00:00:49.215 --> 00:00:51.918 align:start size:94% position:6%
to do to old people?
-Explain Twitter?

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:53.686
I didn´t explain it to this guy.

00:00:53.686 --> 00:00:55.155 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:55.155 --> 00:00:57.924 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That´s not on you.
-That´s not me.

00:00:57.924 --> 00:01:01.895
-So I also want to ask --
You ran as a progressive.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:04.230 size:81% position:41%
And there seems to be
this same conversation

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:08.201
that we had in the 2016 election
about the Democratic party,

00:01:08.201 --> 00:01:10.203 size:88% position:44%
and the progressive wing
and the moderate wing.

00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:12.672
And you have somebody like
Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez,

00:01:12.672 --> 00:01:14.441 align:start size:94% position:6%
where moderate Democrats
are saying

00:01:14.441 --> 00:01:15.809
there´s a very scary thing.

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:18.445
We have to be careful
about the progressive Democrats

00:01:18.445 --> 00:01:20.080 size:81% position:41%
taking over the party.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.182
Where do you stand on that,
as a progressive yourself?

00:01:22.182 --> 00:01:24.684
-So I actually think this
is a totally false choice.

00:01:24.684 --> 00:01:27.120
You know, what I write about
in "Outside the Wire" is that,

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.655
it´s really about going out

00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:29.956 align:start size:94% position:6%
and trying to convince people
of things,

00:01:29.956 --> 00:01:32.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it has been to be
what you genuinely believe.

00:01:32.125 --> 00:01:34.394 size:84% position:42%
Voters will forgive you
for believing something

00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:36.629
they don´t believe as long as
they know you really believe it

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:38.832 align:start size:94% position:6%
and you believe it
´cause you care about them.

00:01:38.832 --> 00:01:40.834 align:start size:94% position:6%
When I was running
for the Senate in ´16,

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:44.070
I would be in Kansas City,
talking about equal pay,

00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:45.472
like a Planned Parenthood event.

00:01:45.472 --> 00:01:48.641
And then I´d go to a rural area
a couple hours later

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:50.276 align:start size:94% position:6%
and be a room full
of middle-class men,

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:52.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I would -- I would still
talk about equal pay.

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:54.447 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I would point out to them,
like, if their wife

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:56.683
is making less money than a man
doing the same job,

00:01:56.683 --> 00:01:58.985 align:start size:94% position:6%
they both have less money
to send their kid to college.

00:01:58.985 --> 00:02:01.688 size:75% position:38%
So you win arguments
by making arguments.

00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:04.424
You don´t win arguments by
trying to make people believe

00:02:04.424 --> 00:02:05.892 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you actually believe
the same thing.

00:02:05.892 --> 00:02:07.827
Like, you can´t win an argument
with your roommate that way,

00:02:07.827 --> 00:02:10.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
let alone the electorate.
-Right.

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:13.066
You also believe a false choice
that seems to be

00:02:13.066 --> 00:02:15.301
what Democrats
are talking about right now is,

00:02:15.301 --> 00:02:19.139 align:start size:94% position:6%
do you choose
to try to flip Trump voters

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:20.874 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the Democratic Party,
especially ones

00:02:20.874 --> 00:02:22.942 align:start size:94% position:6%
that were Democrats
before he came on the scene,

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:24.978
or do you just ignore them

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:27.714 align:start size:94% position:6%
and try to get voters
who haven´t voted before?

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:29.249 align:start size:94% position:6%
Do you also believe
that´s a false choice?

00:02:29.249 --> 00:02:32.018
-Well, yeah, because, again, you
win arguments by making them.

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:34.521 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, like,
I learned this really early.

00:02:34.521 --> 00:02:35.989 align:start size:94% position:6%
I tell a story in the book
about how

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:37.724 align:start size:94% position:6%
one of the very first doors
I ever knocked on

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:39.492 align:start size:94% position:6%
when I was running
for the state legislature,

00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:41.361 size:84% position:42%
this fella came out,
we talked for a second.

00:02:41.361 --> 00:02:44.030
It was really clear,
we did not agree on this issue.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.899 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I kind of
panicked a little inside,

00:02:45.899 --> 00:02:49.269 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I started to do what
I´d seen politicians on TV do.

00:02:49.269 --> 00:02:50.403
So I start trying to spin him,

00:02:50.403 --> 00:02:52.205 align:start size:94% position:6%
make him think
we believe the same thing,

00:02:52.205 --> 00:02:54.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it wasn´t working,
and, finally, I just was like,

00:02:54.407 --> 00:02:55.742
"I got to get out of here."

00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:57.877 align:start size:94% position:6%
So I said, you know, "Thanks.
That´s what I believe.

00:02:57.877 --> 00:02:59.179 size:78% position:39%
I guess we disagree."

00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:01.147 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I go to walk off,
and he´s like, "That´s fair.

00:03:01.147 --> 00:03:03.149
I´ll vote for you.
You can put a sign in my yard."

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:04.851 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
And so, like,

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:07.153
what I learned from that is,

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:08.721 align:start size:94% position:6%
the reason
it´s a false choice is, like,

00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:10.423 align:start size:94% position:6%
the people
who already agree with you,

00:03:10.423 --> 00:03:12.625 align:start size:94% position:6%
they can see that you go out
and make your argument

00:03:12.625 --> 00:03:13.826 size:81% position:41%
to people who may not,

00:03:13.826 --> 00:03:15.261 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they appreciate that,
and they get excited.

00:03:15.261 --> 00:03:16.763 align:start size:94% position:6%
They want to go
knock on doors for you.

00:03:16.763 --> 00:03:19.199
And these people over here who
don´t currently agree with you,

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:22.001
like, if you make your argument,
you might convince some of them.

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:23.536
And if you just try and pretend

00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:24.771 align:start size:94% position:6%
you believe
the same thing as them,

00:03:24.771 --> 00:03:26.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
they´re gonna be like,
"I don´t buy it."

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:28.308
So, like, now, I´m running
for mayor of Kansas City,

00:03:28.308 --> 00:03:30.410 align:start size:94% position:6%
and just like
any other campaign I´ve had,

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:31.744
I´m out there talking to folks

00:03:31.744 --> 00:03:33.646 align:start size:94% position:6%
about what´s going on
on their street.

00:03:33.646 --> 00:03:36.716
I´m not pretending we live
on the same street, you know?

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:39.919
I´m like, "Hey,
I´m seeing a lady in a few days

00:03:39.919 --> 00:03:41.521
who has a possum problem."

00:03:41.521 --> 00:03:43.923
Like, I´m gonna go walk around,
we´re gonna look at that.

00:03:43.923 --> 00:03:45.058 align:start size:94% position:6%
It´s important.

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:46.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You´re gonna look
at the possum problem?

00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:48.094 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Absolutely.
We got to try and fix it.

00:03:48.094 --> 00:03:49.495
Government is about trying
to solve people´s problems.

00:03:49.495 --> 00:03:50.863 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I mean, I thought politicians
had the most boring lives.

00:03:50.863 --> 00:03:52.865 align:start size:94% position:6%
That sounds kick-ass!
-Come with me!

00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:56.502 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I´m down.
I´ll bring my possum gloves.

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:57.737 align:start size:94% position:6%
-If you have some,
I´d like to borrow them.

00:03:57.737 --> 00:03:58.905 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:01.040 align:start size:94% position:6%
No, but, like,
you know, we joke about it,

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.910
but that´s what the best
argument for progressive values

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.246
is progress that people can see,
you know?

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:08.815 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so, in my town,
we´ve had awesome progress,

00:04:08.815 --> 00:04:10.149 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I want to make sure
everybody in town

00:04:10.149 --> 00:04:11.918 align:start size:94% position:6%
can see it and feel it.
That´s why I decided to run.

00:04:11.918 --> 00:04:16.256
-You -- You´ve always criticized
the idea of money in politics,

00:04:16.256 --> 00:04:18.324
getting it out of politics,
and when you were running --

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:19.659
when you were raising money,

00:04:19.659 --> 00:04:22.862
your opponent started doing this
and bringing this

00:04:22.862 --> 00:04:24.330
to your rallies, your events.

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:26.399 align:start size:94% position:6%
This was called
the Kander Pander.

00:04:26.399 --> 00:04:27.400 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.168 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And it was a guy
in a panda suit.

00:04:29.168 --> 00:04:31.771 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Which is what
I like to call a stretch.

00:04:31.771 --> 00:04:33.573 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:33.573 --> 00:04:36.976
So was the Kander Pander
something that you were aware of

00:04:36.976 --> 00:04:38.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
and did you have
to plan around it?

00:04:38.411 --> 00:04:39.612 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah,
I thought it was hilarious.

00:04:39.612 --> 00:04:40.913 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:42.815
-I tell a story in the book
how we had to have, like,

00:04:42.815 --> 00:04:45.618 align:start size:94% position:6%
routes of ingress and egress
to go around it.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:46.853 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know,
which is a military term.

00:04:46.853 --> 00:04:48.187 align:start size:94% position:6%
that, clearly,
no one here knows.

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:50.590 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
But now you do.

00:04:50.590 --> 00:04:53.826
And so what was amazing to me
was there was a dude who --

00:04:53.826 --> 00:04:55.395 align:start size:94% position:6%
That was his job.
-Yeah.

00:04:55.395 --> 00:04:57.330
-And it was hot sometimes.

00:04:57.330 --> 00:05:00.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
And what I do respect about it
was that, one time,

00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:03.136 align:start size:94% position:6%
a group of, like, I think,
kindergartners came,

00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:05.004 align:start size:94% position:6%
and all they knew -- they
didn´t know it was politics.

00:05:05.004 --> 00:05:06.372 align:start size:94% position:6%
They just thought,
"There´s a panda."

00:05:06.372 --> 00:05:07.674 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-And, like, it was hot,

00:05:07.674 --> 00:05:09.208
but he stayed and took pictures
with all of them.

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:10.410 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That´s really nice.
-Yeah.

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:11.978 size:81% position:41%
-The thing that seems
really cruel to me is,

00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:13.913
they make him wear a panda suit,
and then they just, like,

00:05:13.913 --> 00:05:15.648 align:start size:94% position:6%
go to Kinkos and print out
Kander Pander.

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:16.916
-Yeah, that´s a good point.

00:05:16.916 --> 00:05:18.384
They got to put
some production value in there.

00:05:18.384 --> 00:05:19.886 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, he could´ve been like,
"Can´t I have one

00:05:19.886 --> 00:05:22.255
that goes around my neck so I
don´t have to hold it all day?"

00:05:22.255 --> 00:05:24.023
-The point of all it, though,

00:05:24.023 --> 00:05:25.458 align:start size:94% position:6%
is how insane
the system is, right?

00:05:25.458 --> 00:05:27.794
I mean, the fact that, like,
I had to go all over the country

00:05:27.794 --> 00:05:29.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
raising money
for a senate race in Missouri,

00:05:29.362 --> 00:05:30.997 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you have
to raise so much money.

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:32.098 align:start size:94% position:6%
At one point --
Like, you would --

00:05:32.098 --> 00:05:33.366
It was like speed dating.

00:05:33.366 --> 00:05:36.936
You would sit in these meetings,
and I had this fear.

00:05:36.936 --> 00:05:38.271 align:start size:94% position:6%
It wasn´t even fear,
it was a reality.

00:05:38.271 --> 00:05:40.006 align:start size:94% position:6%
Halfway through, sometimes,
I would forget the name

00:05:40.006 --> 00:05:41.974 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the person,
but they didn´t know.

00:05:41.974 --> 00:05:43.843 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I was afraid
that they were gonna be like,

00:05:43.843 --> 00:05:45.111 align:start size:94% position:6%
"What´s my name?"
[ Laughter ]

00:05:45.111 --> 00:05:47.947 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, one day,
this dude says to me --

00:05:47.947 --> 00:05:50.149 align:start size:94% position:6%
This very nice man
who helped the campaign.

00:05:50.149 --> 00:05:53.219
He says to me, he says,
"Do you know John Smith?"

00:05:53.219 --> 00:05:54.620
And I´m in my head, like,

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:57.290 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I really thought
this was John Smith."

00:05:57.290 --> 00:05:58.991 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I´m like,
"Oh, my God, it´s happening!"

00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:01.561
And so I didn´t know what to do,
so I just bit the bullet,

00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:03.629 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I´m like,
"Aren´t you John Smith?"

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:05.565
and he just acted like that´s
the dumbest thing he ever heard.

00:06:05.565 --> 00:06:07.300 align:start size:94% position:6%
He´s like, "I´m talking about
the other John Smith."

00:06:07.300 --> 00:06:09.068 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:10.737 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I´m like so relieved
when he said that.

00:06:10.737 --> 00:06:13.673
I start just laughing like the
joker, just like cracking up.

00:06:13.673 --> 00:06:15.808 align:start size:94% position:6%
Anyway, I walked out of there
with a campaign check

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:17.243 align:start size:94% position:6%
and an introduction
to the other John Smith.

00:06:17.243 --> 00:06:18.311 align:start size:94% position:6%
-There you go.
That works out perfectly.

00:06:18.311 --> 00:06:19.379 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Worked out great.
Yeah.

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:20.446 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Hey, thanks so much
for being here.

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:21.681
Best of luck with everything.

